\section{Setting up the diagnostic quizzes, literately}
\label{app:diagnostics}

This appendix is a literate program: the prose and the code are one and
the same source, woven into the text you are reading and tangled into
the files the computer runs.
It implements the diagnostic instruments for the learning-study
evaluation motivated in \cref{conclusions}: pre- and post-tests at the
level of the whole course, and pre- and post-tests around each taught
topic.
Fourteen files are tangled from this section: the course-level pair
[[quiz-course-start.json]] and [[quiz-course-end.json]], and a
start--end pair per topic
([[quiz-funcvars-start.json]]/[[quiz-funcvars-end.json]] and
correspondingly for [[conditionals]], [[repetitions]], [[types]],
[[classes]] and [[debugging]]).
The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions,
for example:
\begin{minted}{bash}
for f in quiz-*.json; do
  canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f "$f"
done
\end{minted}

The items are where the catalogue pays off.
Each item targets one critical aspect from the analyses in
\cref{misconceptions}, and --- this is the diagnostic value --- each
distractor encodes a \emph{catalogued misconception}: a student who
picks it does not merely answer wrongly, they tell us which
misconception they hold.
This is the construction principle of concept inventories
\autocite{Julie2020ConceptInventory,KaltakciGurel2015Diagnostics},
here with the distractors grounded in the catalogue rather than
collected anew.

\subsection{Parallel forms and counterbalancing}

Measuring the same student twice with the same question risks
measuring recognition of the question rather than understanding of the
aspect.
Every aspect therefore has \emph{two} parallel items, A and B: the
critical aspect and the encoded misconceptions are invariant, and only
the surface varies --- names, values, and which choice is correct.
The pre-test uses form A for the odd-numbered aspects and form B for
the even-numbered; the post-test uses the complement.
Both forms thus appear in both administrations, so a difficulty
difference between the forms affects the pre- and post-scores
symmetrically and cancels in the gain.

The design we would prefer is for Canvas to draw one of the two forms
at random \emph{per student} at each administration, which controls
form effects per item rather than per test.
That is not yet automatable: New Quizzes item banks cannot be created
through the Canvas API, and Classic Quizzes' question groups (which do
support a random draw) are not yet supported by [[canvaslms]].
The counterbalanced fixed forms below are the strongest design the
current tooling allows; because each form is its own chunk, switching
to a random draw later only changes the quiz descriptions, not the
items.
% TODO(#29): upgrade to per-student random draw when canvaslms
% supports Classic question groups (upstream feature request filed).

\subsection{The course-level quizzes}

The course-level pair brackets the whole course: the start quiz runs in
the first week, the end quiz in the last.
The end quiz opens with a question of its own: whether the student
prepared for the quiz.
We ask because preparation would confound the gain measure --- a
student who reviews the material the night before measures
preparation, not what the course left behind.
Each item's position encodes its topic (tens digit) and aspect (ones
digit), so positions are unique and stable across all fourteen
quizzes; Canvas tolerates the gaps.

<<[[quiz-course-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Concept quiz: what you know already",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz checks which programming concepts you already know, so that we can adapt the course. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers&mdash;the course will teach you those. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 22,
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<graded result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<pre items>>
  ]
}
@

<<[[quiz-course-end.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Concept quiz: what you know now",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz mirrors the concept quiz from the start of the course, so that we can see what you have learned during the course. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without preparation, help or tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 22,
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<graded result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<preparation item>>,
    <<post items>>
  ]
}
@

The course items are the union of the topic banks, in each form.

<<pre items>>=
<<funcvars pre items>>,
<<conditionals pre items>>,
<<repetitions pre items>>,
<<types pre items>>,
<<classes pre items>>,
<<debugging pre items>>
@

<<post items>>=
<<funcvars post items>>,
<<conditionals post items>>,
<<repetitions post items>>,
<<types post items>>,
<<classes post items>>,
<<debugging post items>>
@

All quizzes are diagnostics: there is no time limit, a single attempt
suffices, and answers are never shuffled, since the order of the
choices is meaningful in several items (the misconception-encoding
distractor should not accidentally move next to the correct answer).
Students see their score but never the correct answers --- the items
must stay reusable for the later administrations.

<<scheduling settings>>=
"time_limit": null,
"due_at": null,
"unlock_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
@

<<interaction settings>>=
"shuffle_answers": false,
"shuffle_questions": false,
"has_time_limit": false,
@

<<graded result view>>=
"display_item_response_correctness": false,
"display_item_correct_answer": false,
"display_points_awarded": true,
"display_points_possible": true
@

The preparation item is ungraded and sits first in every end quiz.

<<preparation item>>=
{
  "position": 1,
  "points_possible": 0,
  "entry": {
    "title": "Preparation",
    "item_body": "<p>Did you prepare for this quiz&mdash;for example, review the course material&mdash;before taking it?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
    "interaction_data": {
      "choices": [
        {"position": 1, "item_body": "<p>No</p>"},
        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>Yes, briefly</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>Yes, thoroughly</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 1},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

\subsection{The topic quizzes}

Each topic pair brackets one teaching unit in the learning study: the
start quiz runs immediately before the unit, the end quiz immediately
