\subsection{Functions and variables (positions 11--17)}

Seven aspects from the functions-and-variables analyses.
Pre-test forms: A for the odd aspects, B for the even; post-test the
complement.

<<funcvars pre items>>=
<<item 11A: adding one to a variable>>,
<<item 12B: a sum computed earlier>>,
<<item 13A: a function is defined>>,
<<item 14B: calling with no arguments>>,
<<item 15A: where the returned value goes>>,
<<item 16B: a list passed to a function>>,
<<item 17A: calling before defining>>
@

<<funcvars post items>>=
<<item 11B: adding two to a variable>>,
<<item 12A: a sum computed earlier>>,
<<item 13B: a function is defined>>,
<<item 14A: calling with no arguments>>,
<<item 15B: where the returned value goes>>,
<<item 16A: a list passed to a function>>,
<<item 17B: calling before defining>>
@

\paragraph{Aspect 11: assignment is not an equation.}
The critical aspect is that \mintinline{python}{=} assigns --- computes
the right-hand side from the current values and stores the result ---
rather than states an equation
\autocite{Kohn2017VariableEvaluation,Plass2015Variables}.
The \enquote{no solution} distractor encodes the equation reading,
which the abstract of \textcite{Kohn2017VariableEvaluation} reports
verbatim as \enquote{believed to be outright impossible}; the
expression distractor encodes storing the calculation instead of its
value; the unchanged-value distractor encodes reading the second
assignment as inert.
Form B varies the name, the values and the correct position; the three
encoded misconceptions are the same.

<<item 11A: adding one to a variable>>=
